# Python Web Application Development Standards

## Code Style

- Follow PEP 8 guidelines with 4 spaces for indentation
- Limit lines to 79 characters for code, 72 for comments
- Use blank lines strategically: 2 before top-level functions/classes, 1 between methods
- Organize imports in groups: standard library, third-party, local application
- Avoid wildcard imports (from module import *)
- Use absolute imports rather than relative imports

## Naming Conventions

- Modules/Packages: lowercase with underscores (user_authentication)
- Classes: CapWords/PascalCase (UserAuthentication)
- Functions/Methods/Variables: lowercase with underscores (calculate_total)
- Constants: ALL_UPPERCASE with underscores (MAX_CONNECTIONS)
- Private attributes: prefix with single underscore (_internal_method)
- "Private" class members: prefix with double underscores (__private_method)
- Use descriptive names that convey purpose

## Project Structure

```
project_root/
├── src/                    # Source code
│   ├── __init__.py
│   ├── main.py             # Application entry point
│   ├── config/             # Configuration files
│   ├── api/                # API endpoints
│   ├── models/             # Data models
│   ├── services/           # Business logic
│   ├── utils/              # Utility functions
│   └── templates/          # Templates for web views
├── tests/                  # Test suite
├── docs/                   # Documentation
├── static/                 # Static files (CSS, JS, images)
├── requirements.txt        # Project dependencies
└── README.md               # Project overview
```

## Function Design

- Functions should do one thing and do it well
- Keep functions under 40 lines
- Use type hints for parameters and return values
- Break complex operations into smaller functions
- Implement appropriate error handling
- Use default values for optional parameters

## Documentation

- Use Google style docstrings for all modules, classes, functions, and methods
- Include: purpose summary, detailed description, parameters, return values, exceptions, examples
- Write comments for complex algorithms or business rules
- Comments should explain why, not what
- Include code examples in docstrings when appropriate

## Error Handling

- Catch specific exceptions, not broad exception handlers
- Include context in error messages
- Re-raise exceptions when appropriate
- Use custom exceptions for application-specific errors
- Don't use exceptions for flow control

## Logging

- Use Python's built-in logging module
- Apply appropriate log levels (DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR, CRITICAL)
- Include relevant context in log messages
- Avoid logging sensitive information
- Configure logging appropriately for different environments

## Testing

- Write tests for all functionality including edge cases
- Organize tests to mirror application structure
- Name test files with test_ prefix
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert pattern
- Tests should be independent and idempotent

## Security

- Validate and sanitize all user input
- Follow authentication/authorization best practices
- Securely store passwords using strong hashing
- Encrypt sensitive data at rest and in transit
- Follow relevant data protection regulations

## Performance

- Use efficient algorithms and data structures
- Optimize database interactions (indexes, query minimization)
- Consider asynchronous programming for I/O-bound operations
- Implement caching where appropriate
- Use generators and iterators for large data sets

## Version Control

- Write meaningful commit messages (concise summary, detailed explanation)
- Reference issue numbers in commit messages
- Keep changes focused and cohesive
- Document complex logic
- Handle edge cases and test thoroughly
